# Java SDK End to End Tests

End-to-end tests live in the module whose code they test, not in `sentry-samples`. Samples have no
tests. A test runs the code the way an app would and asserts what reaches Sentry over the real HTTP
transport.

## MockSentryServer

`MockSentryServer` (`sentry-test-support`) is an in-process HTTP server on a free port. Point the
SDK at `server.dsn`, then:

- `awaitEnvelope(timeoutMillis) { predicate }` polls for a matching envelope and fails with what it
  received on timeout.
- `assertNoEnvelope(millis) { predicate }` checks that nothing matching arrives.
- `MockSentryServer { ... }` takes a hook that runs before each check while waiting, for example to
  flush an OpenTelemetry tracer provider.

`EnvelopeMatchers.kt` has the accessors and matchers: `event()`, `transaction()`, `logs()`,
`metrics()`, `profileChunk()`, `hasSpanWithOp`, `hasFlag`, `hasLogWithAttribute`, `hasMetric` and
so on.

Call `Sentry.flush()` before awaiting when Sentry runs in the test's JVM: logs and metrics are
batched. Envelopes that arrive late can outlive a server's `reset()`, so tests that share a server
match on values only they produce.

## Where the tests are

- **Core and plain integrations** use their module's `src/test`. Examples: `SentryEndToEndTest`
  (`sentry`), `SentryAppenderIntegrationTest` (`sentry-logback`, `sentry-log4j2`, loading the
  appender from XML), `SentryHandlerIntegrationTest` (`sentry-jul`),
  `ContinuousProfilerEndToEndTest` (`sentry-async-profiler`), `SentryKafkaEndToEndTest`
  (`sentry-kafka`), `AgentlessEndToEndTest` and `OtlpEndToEndTest` (`sentry-opentelemetry-*`).
- **Spring Boot** (`sentry-spring-boot`, `sentry-spring-boot-jakarta`, `sentry-spring-boot-4`) has
  one JVM test suite per flavor, because a flavor is a classpath: Sentry's auto-configuration and
  OpenTelemetry mode switch on which classes are present.
  - `integrationTest`: servlet app.
  - `integrationTestWebflux`: reactive app.
  - `integrationTestOtelNoAgent`: `sentry-opentelemetry-agentless-spring`.
  - `integrationTestOtelAgent` and `integrationTestOtelAgentWithoutAutoInit`: the built agent.
  - `integrationTestOtlp` (Boot 4): `sentry-opentelemetry-otlp-spring`.
  - `integrationTestLog4j2` (Boot 3, 4): Log4j2 instead of Logback, for the auto-configured
    Log4j2 appender.

  Each suite has a base class with `@SpringBootTest(RANDOM_PORT)` and a `@DynamicPropertySource`
  that sets `sentry.dsn`. Subclasses share one context and one server.
- **Plain Spring** (`sentry-spring`, `sentry-spring-jakarta`, `sentry-spring-7`) has an
  `integrationTest` suite that starts the app in an embedded Tomcat, the way a WAR starts. Spring
  Boot stays off its classpath, because `SentrySpringServletContainerInitializer` behaves
  differently with it.

## The OpenTelemetry agent

The agent must not run in the test's JVM, because it would instrument the test's own HTTP client
and server. The Spring Boot modules have an `agentFixture` source set with the app. `AgentApp`
starts it once per test JVM in a child process, with `ChildJvmApp` and the agent jar passed through
`ChildJvmTestArguments`. The suite's task runs with the agent initializing Sentry, and
`integrationTestOtelAgentWithoutAutoInit` runs the same tests with the Spring Boot starter doing
it. Gradle runs the two in parallel.

## Running them

- The `springBootMatrix` block in each Spring module's build file runs its suites on every version
  of its Spring Boot line in `gradle/spring-boot-versions.json`, with a task per suite and version,
  e.g. `integrationTestWebfluxBoot3_2_12`. The suites and the SDK compile once, against the
  catalog's version; only the runtime classpath changes. `allIntegrationTestsBoot3_2_12` runs one
  version's suites, `allIntegrationTestsBoot3` every version's.
- The `spring-boot-{2,3,4}-matrix.yml` workflows read the same file and run each version in its own
  job, e.g. `./gradlew :sentry-spring-jakarta:allIntegrationTestsBoot3_2_12
  :sentry-spring-boot-jakarta:allIntegrationTestsBoot3_2_12`. The suites aren't part of `check`, so
  a new suite only runs on CI once it's added to its module's `springBootMatrix` block, and a new
  module once it's added to its workflow.
- Suite code may only use APIs that exist in the oldest version of its line. Compiled against a
  newer one, a missing API fails at runtime with `NoSuchMethodError` or `NoClassDefFoundError`.

## Gotchas

- Don't use the context's `TestRestTemplate`. It comes from the `RestTemplateBuilder` that Sentry
  customizes, and that the OpenTelemetry starter instruments, so it adds or replaces trace headers
  on the requests a test sends. Build one outside the context.
- Sentry exports OpenTelemetry spans in batches every 5 seconds. In-process, flush the tracer
  provider from the server's hook. With the agent this isn't possible, so each test waits for a
  batch and negative checks wait at least 6 seconds.
- Sentry is global, so a second live Spring context in an OpenTelemetry suite takes it over. Keep
  one live context per JVM (`@DirtiesContext` when a test needs its own).
- The async profiler rejects a traces path with a `.` anywhere in its absolute form. Put the path
  in `java.io.tmpdir`.
- Kafka uses an in-JVM broker (`@EmbeddedKafka` or spring-kafka-test's broker classes), so no
  Docker is needed.
